WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
How can you find which chromosome spots track a trait like height?

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.280
Adult height forms a smooth bell curve, even though inheritance obeys Mendel's

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:15.820
rules. Many chromosome positions shape height alongside the environment, making it a

4
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:22.040
polygenic trait. Each position is a locus, and its alternative versions are alleles.

5
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.100
A genome-wide association study, or GWAS, scans many genomes for trait-linked

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:37.420
alleles. It counts alleles, not people, since every person carries two copies at

7
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.640
each locus. A chi-square score compares allele counts in people with and without a

8
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.860
disease to counts expected with no link.

9
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.340
The bigger the score, the more surprising the link with the trait.

10
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:56.770
The scan repeats those allele counts across hundreds of thousands of loci at once.

11
00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:03.070
With so many tests, some scores run high by chance, so only signals crossing a

12
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.330
strict threshold count.

13
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.870
Because nearby variants travel together, each measured variant acts as a tag for its

14
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:17.710
region. So genome-wide signals flag associated chromosome regions, not proven causal

15
00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:18.890
variants or genes.

16
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:21.933
Test that idea on a new scan.

17
00:01:23.553 --> 00:01:28.293
Suppose one tag variant rises far above the threshold in people with a disease.

18
00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:34.173
Does that strong peak prove the tag variant causes the disease, or is something

19
00:01:34.173 --> 00:01:36.913
missing? Pause and predict.

20
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:47.857
No: the peak flags a linked region in one study population, not a proven cause.

21
00:01:49.177 --> 00:01:54.517
A shared environment can also mislead, when carriers of an allele also share a diet

22
00:01:54.517 --> 00:02:00.097
or upbringing. And population patterns never assign one person's cause.

23
00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:07.117
To test mechanism, experimenters edit a candidate gene in animals and compare them

24
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:08.357
with unedited controls.

25
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:13.930
Adding up small effects across many variants builds a polygenic score.

26
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.250
Yet every score inherits the limits of its study cohort.

27
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.430
Fahed and colleagues studied UK Biobank, a cohort healthier than the general

28
00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:30.610
population. Their scores were less precise outside the mostly European ancestry

29
00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.170
groups they were built on.

30
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:36.843
Used carefully, genome scans unlock powerful leads.

31
00:02:38.143 --> 00:02:42.723
A 2022 study scanned up to 5.4 million people for height.

32
00:02:44.003 --> 00:02:49.003
It listed 12,111 independent variants associated with height.

33
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:55.863
In 2016, Aswin Sekar, Steven McCarroll and colleagues studied the C4A gene.

34
00:02:55.863 --> 00:03:01.823
Variants predicted to raise its brain expression were associated with higher

35
00:03:01.823 --> 00:03:02.963
schizophrenia risk.

36
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:09.597
Losing C4 in mice reduced synapse pruning in the visual thalamus, making that

37
00:03:09.597 --> 00:03:11.837
association a testable hypothesis.

38
00:03:13.357 --> 00:03:20.557
In 2023, Enbody and colleagues analysed 3,955 Daphne Major finch genomes.

39
00:03:22.237 --> 00:03:27.737
Six large-effect loci together explained 45 percent of beak-size variation in the

40
00:03:27.737 --> 00:03:30.897
medium ground finch, leaving most unassigned.

41
00:03:31.857 --> 00:03:36.357
Genome scans turn traits into maps that point experiments toward causes.
